argJitT -- DOES A JITTED BODY READ AN ACTION'S `argument` AS THE PASSED FIELD?

⚠ PROMOTED INTO THE FLEET 2026-09-05 BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE COST A SILENT
REGRESSION THE SAME DAY. Until this file existed there was NO fixture anywhere
in the tree that read an action's `argument` under the JIT -- every jit fixture
reads globals. Item 3's runAction revision moved the argument bind out of the
jitting arm; the inlined road stopped reading the passed field and answered 0,
and THE FLEET STAYED AT 179 GREEN. It was found by hand, on a probe, by
accident of re-running it. That is the hole this file closes.

WHAT IT DISCRIMINATES. The jitted callee is INLINED, so its body is walked at
EMIT time and the argument must be bound by then -- which is a different road
from jitBindArgRT's run-time bind for a callee given its own function. Both
roads exist and only one of them is asserted here; incant/argRoundJ is the
other, and it is pinned red on jitArgBake.

    8 then 22, oracle 14      the jitted body reads the passed field per fire
    two fires the same        the value was folded, not loaded per fire
    0 / 0                     the jitted body is not reading the passed field
                              at all -- the exact shape of the 2026-09-05
                              regression, and of the pre-B state before it

⚠ TWO FIRES WITH DIFFERENT ANSWERS, NOT MERELY DIFFERENT INPUTS. 8 and 22 differ,
so a folded constant cannot satisfy both; a pair that happened to agree would
prove nothing (the injectivity rule, jitJR's).

⚠ ORACLE LAST, AND IT IS NOT STYLE (bear-trap #25). testing() routes on
isCoded, and an interpreted run consumes it -- so an oracle placed first would
send the jitted half to jitRunIfTest, assert nothing about the JIT, and print a
plausible number the whole way. The oracle reads a FIELD, which is correct in
that position; only a RETURNED value would need its own process.

SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, exactly ONE stop().
